advantage:
there is no credentials stored in the instance(no security  exposer)

Autoscaling in AWS:
	Amazon EC2 autoscaling
		->based on metric like cpu utilization and memory utilization

	->automatically launches and terminates instance.
	->maintain availability and scale capacity.
	->it works with EC2,ECS,EKS

Integrate with many AWS services,including:
	cloudwatch for monitoring and scaling
	elastic load balancing for distributing connections
	Amazon VPC for deploying instances across AZs.

first create the auto scaling group where the number of instances running let say we have
we have auto scaling group and there is 2 AZ
each AZ has 2 ec2 instance running and they are continuously sending the metrics
to cloud watch and
when cpu reaches let say 80% then amazon cloud watch notifies auto scaling group
to add a new node in the auto scaling group

Q:what is elasticity
it means it scale out and and scale back when required.

configuration of auto scaling group :
1:it uses  launch template,it defines the setting we try use
2:launch config
3:click on auto scaling group
	while configuring group size and scaling
		group size:
			under desired type
			desired capacity :2(unit /instance)
		scaling:
		scaling limits
			min desired capacity:2
			max desired capacity:6


health checks :
	1:EC2=EC2 status checks
	2:ELB=Uses the ELB health checks in addition to EC2 status checks

health check grace period:
->how long to wait before checking the health status of the instance

Types of Auto scaling:
	1:manual:make changes to ASG(auto scaling group) size manually
	{manual adding the number of instance you want deployed in the auto scaling group}

	2:dynamic:automatically scales based on demand.

	3:predictive:uses machine learning to predict

	4:scheduled:scales based on schedule.


Amazon ELB(elastic load balancer):
	so load balancer provides high availability and fault tolerance.
	essentially there are single end point,so there are single DNS name or IP address
	behind which a bunch of different instance sit.
	so it's gonna automatically distribute connections to those EC2 instance.
	Target include:
		->amazon ec2 instance
		->amazon ecs containers
		->ip addresses
		->lambda function
		->other load balancers
	we can chain them together also

if any instance goes down than elb trigger the health check if down then it
redistributed the request among rest up instance
it also notified the auto scaling group that this instance is not responding to health,
then auto scaning will terminate and replace them

auto scanning is going to distribute the load across multiple availability zone and
load balancers sitting in the front is then gonna
distribute connection to all the instance

types of load balancer :
	1:application load balancer(it understand information in the http and https headers)
		->operates at the request level
		->routes based on the content of request (L7)
		->supports path based routing ,host-based routing,query string parameter-based
		    routing and source ip address -based routing
		->supports instances,ip addresses,,lambda functions and containers as targets

	use case:
		->web application with L7 routing(http,https)
		->microservice architecture(eg->docker,containers)
		->lambda targets

	2:network level load balancers:this is connection level(tcp,udp,tls,tcp_udp)
	  load balancers
		->operates on the connection level
		->routes connection based on ip protocol data(L4)
		->offers high ultra performance,low latency and TLS offloading at scale.
		->we can have static ip/elastic ip(means you can hardcode them and white list
		    them in firewalls)
		->supports UDP and static ip addresses as targets

	use case:
		->Tcp and Udp based application
		->require low latency
		->static ip addresses
		->vpc endpoint services

	3:gateway load balancer:
		->exchange traffic with appliances using GENEVE protocol on port 6081.
		->operates on layers 3:listens to all packets on all ports.

	use case:
		->deploy,scale and manage 3rd party virtual network appliances
		->centralised inspection and monitoring
		->it is used for virtual networking appliances such as firewalls,
		    intrusion detection systems,intrusion prevention systems.


How to create load balancer
	->create a target group
	->target group is going to contain the instances
	->choose target type
	->choose rest

	->now click on load balancer and choose which load balancer you want
	let say application load balancer .click on create.
	while creating 1 important thing is that you have to define the listeners and
	routing
	so when the request come to 80 it forward the request to target group
	(where instance are attached)

how to set up automatic load balancing
	->go to ASG group-> load balancing ->edit->select target group
	    ->go to target group and verifies
	->go to load balancers and test it.
	->copy the dns url and paste in web browser you can the instance running.

How to create scaling policy
	go to auto scaling group -> go to automatic scaling
	    ->create dynamic scaling policies
	->go to cloud watch ->check alarm -all alarm ->we can now see these 2 target
	    tracking


Amazon storage services:
	3 different types of storage systems
		1:block based
		2:file based
		3:object based

amazon elastic block store
amazon elastic file systems
amazon simple storage service(s3)

1:Block based Storage:
	HDD(hard dish drive)
		:also known as magnetic drives
		:older technology
		:much slower than SSD
		:much cheaper than SSD
		:the os read and write at the block level
		:disk can be internal or network attached
		ex:in window systems(OS) sees a volume .A volume can be partioned and formatted
			let say in window we can see c drive(800GB) and d drive (200GB)
	SSD(solid state drive
		:uses flash memory(like cheap) not moving
		:newer technology
		:much faster than HDD
		:more expensive than HDD
		:better performance

2:File based storage:
		->a filesystem is mounted to OS using a network share,
            any number of system is mounted to network and can read and write
            to the network.
		->in file based there is somewhere there is block based storage systems.
		    this is attached with network attach server(NAS)
		->there is network location (z)

3:Object storage systems
		->cannot be mounted but can be accessed programmatically using rest apis.
		(which is advantage for programmer how can write the code for upload download
		 and delete
		->we can scale easily and having lower cost.
		->here we have object storage containers
		->here the object is any type file like videos,images,word documents,PDFS
		->s3 is the object storage systems in the AWS.
		->bucket
		->here user upload objects using web browser or the apis
		->the protocol which is used here is generally the HTTp protocol using
		    rest apis

Amazon EBS(elastic block store) and Instance stores
	->block based storage system.
	->EBS volumes is exit within an AZ
	->the volume is attached over a network
	->**your volume should be present in the same AZ
	->**the volume is automatically replicated within the AZ.

	How can we take backup:
		->we can take snapshot(point in time)
		->** snapshot is not storing in the availability zone(AZ)
		    because it is exactly on Amazon s3 and is regional service.
		->we can create  multiple snapshot and snapshot are incremental
		    (snapshot1.0,1.1,1.2)

	how can you take backup in different AZ.
	->take snapshot from the  AZ than you can create an EBS Volume in another AZ
	    from  snapshot
	2nd way
	->you can also create an AMI from snapshot.
	    (a new instance can be created within the same region.

Instance store volume:
	->are ephemeral in nature(non persistent)
	->instance store volumes are physically attached to the host and
	    offer high performance.
	->if power is off to the host server ,all the data is gone forever.
	use case->temporary data which can be recreated or will come in near future.

practical create an attach an EBS volume.
	->go to elastic block store and create volume which types you want.
	->you can attach this volume to any instance you want,simply go to that instance
	    and this volume.one note here you cannot attached the volume
	in different AGs.

	how to create the snapshot:
	->go to volume ->select the volume ->actions->create snapshot
	->go to snapshot and verify it (note these snap are stored in s3 bucket)
	->how to create the same snapshot in other AZ,
		->lets create a volume from this snapshot using the actions menu and
		    its gonna be
			->now attach this volume

Amazon machine Images(AMI) and the associated snapshot
	->instance was launched from a custom AMI
	->the AMI has been used to launch additional identical instances
	->deregister after the use

Amazon Elastic file system(EFS):
	->is the shared file system.
	->we can connect instances to EPS file systems from multiple availability zone(AZ).
	    (connection protocol is NFS
	->EFS is regional based.
	->can only be used with linux not operating system.
	->Data consistency:write operations for regional file systems are
	    durably stored across availability zones.
	->file locking:NFS client applications can use NFS v4 file locking for read
	    and write operations on EFS files for data consistency.
	->storage classes:there are 3 options
		1:EFS Standard ->uses SSD for low latency performance.
		2:EFS infrequent access (IA)->cost effective options.used when you don't need
		    the data regularly.
		3:EFS Archive->even cheaper for less active data.once or twice in a year.
	->durability:all storage classes offer 11 9s of durability.
	    like s3(very unlikely you gonna loose your data)

	you can create EFS system in 1 region and replicate it to other region for
	    disaster recovery also the replicas are only the read only.

	EPS replication :data is replicate across regions for disaster recovery
	    purposes with RPO /RTO in minutes
	Automatic backup:EPS integrates with AWS backup for automatic file system backups.

	performance options:there 2 options
		1:provided throughput ->specify a level of throughput that the file system
		    can drive independent of the file system size.
		2:Bursting throughput-> throughput scales with the amount of storage
		    and supports bursting to higher levels.

